   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.1. Increased capacity of the member States to ensure respect for, promote and realize fundamental principles and rights at work</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The second phase of the National Policy for the Elimination of Child Labour and the National Action Plan (NAP) for the elimination of child labour (2021-2025) were validated in April 2021 and are being implemented.  Prior to developing the NAP, the Federal Ministry of Labour and Employment, in collaboration with members of the tripartite plus National Steering Committee on the elimination of child labour (NSCCL), evaluated the implementation of the 2013-2017 National Action Plan for the elimination of child labour. The evaluation report which was validated in November 2020,, informed the review of the policy and development of the new NAP. 
In addition, two surveys on Situational Analysis of the prevalence of Child Labour in the Artisanal Small Scale Gold Mining Sector and Situational Analysis of the prevalence of Child Labour in the Cocoa Sector were conducted and validated by members of the NSCCL and the States steering committees on the elimination of child labour (SSCCL), in October 2020. The studies identified 1440 boys and girls and 500 women and men as direct beneficiaries for livelihood, education, and economic empowerment. 
The SSCCL in Ondo and Niger States were re-established. 
11 Community Child labour Monitoring Committees (CCLMC) were established in Ondo (7 committees) and Niger (4 committees) States. Following their establishment a training program was conducted in April 2021, for 116 Members of the CCLMC including Agric Extension officers and Mine Inspectors to more effectively advocate, monitor and report on child labour cases in the ASGM and cocoa sectors. 
A baseline study on Strengthening Social Dialogue Mechanisms and Practice to Eliminate Child Labour in the Cocoa and Artisanal Small Scale Gold Mining Global Supply Chains (GSCs) in Nigeria conducted. The ILO, with the support of the Netherlands Government, has:

- provided the technical support for the evaluation, development and validation of the National Policy and its National Action Plan in April 2021. 
- organized and coordinated the various workshops including the above stated validation meetings. 
- supported the compilation of the various legal frameworks for the elimination of child labour and child protection in Nigeria. The document will be finalized before the end of 2021 afterwards it will be simplified and translated into the indigenous language
-  supported the training and meetings leading to the re-establishment of the SSCCL.
- Signed Implementation Agreements   with the Nigeria Employers Consultative Association NECA and other institutions, which conducted studies and  other activities focussed on strengthening the private sector in the elimination of child labour. The ILO also provided technical inputs into the studies and coordinated the virtual validation meeting held on the 15th and 29th of October 2020.
-  coordinated and facilitated the workshops organized for establishment and capacity building of the CCLMC members and supported the development of a Terms of Reference to guide the interventions of the CCLMC within their communities;
- supported the National Steering Committee on Child Labour to review the Nigerian Labour standards Bill in line with the observations of Committee of Experts on the Application of Conventions and Recommendations; and 
- is supporting the development and adoption of the NAP to the situational context of child labour in 6 States (Ondo, Niger, Osun, Lagos, Oyo, and Ogun). Thus, the ILO is supporting additional 6 Action Plans for the elimination of child labour in Nigeria. (work in progress)
- as well as a  baseline study and providing technical support to strengthen social dialogue in the elimination of child labour in Nigeria.</narrative>
   </description>
